ion when its  subcommand is used. By contrast, Itsaplan runner starts the selected CLI in configured ; worktree and branch behavior for an external runner are not supplied by its runner contract. [Sources: ; upstream README URL above]\\\\n- #4142 is an open, separate feature about making Itsaplan a  via REST/MCP. Neither #4142 nor #4143 declares a dependency; source reviewed here does not establish whether the backend item is required for external-run dispatch. [Source: https://github.com/Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills/issues/4142]\\\\n\\\\n### Cross-Angle Signals\\\\n\\\\n| Finding | Angles | Confidence |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| An Itsaplan runner is an operator-machine queue consumer; development-harness dispatch is currently a local subprocess/PID/result-file lifecycle. | API State + Ecosystem + Codebase Audit | High |\\\\n| Upstream run identity (, agent key, optional Itsaplan issue) and local identity (, , GitHub issue) are different, and no mapping is defined. | API State + Codebase Audit | High |\\\\n| Completion semantics differ: upstream records reported success/failed outcome; local code records a nonempty result file as completion and separately checks PID liveness. | API State + Codebase Audit | High |\\\\n\\\\n### Conflicts\\\\n\\\\n[RESOLVED] The target phrase  conflates product and package versions: current release evidence identifies the product as v1.5.0 and its runner package as v0.5.3. No behavioral-source conflict was found.\\\\n\\\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\\\n\\\\n- No source-backed, supported operation to enqueue one specifically identified external-agent run from a development-harness wave/item was established.\\\\n- No authoritative mapping is recorded for development-harness milestone/wave/GitHub issue versus Itsaplan project/issue/agent/run identities.\\\\n- No operator/instance facts establish the runner machine, agent key owner, enabled MCP project, coding-agent sign-in, or permitted unattended permission posture.\\\\n- No decision establishes the external runner repository/worktree/branch boundary.\\\\n\\\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\\\n\\\\n- Local static source exposes no cancellation endpoint or explicit retry operation for ; stale recovery is PID-based.\\\\n- No quantitative end-to-end benchmark or payload-size baseline for a specific #4143 change was available.\\\\n- #4142 necessity for #4143 is not established by either issue.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-10-17T07:24:51.778566Z</sub>\\n\\n### Research \u2014 Itsaplan v1.5.0 / @itsaplan/runner v0.5.3 and dh dispatch spawn \u2014 2026-10-17\\n\\nResearch method: four independent angles: API state, ecosystem and official docs, quantitative impact, and local codebase audit. Cross-angle and conflict review completed. This records observed behavior and explicit gaps only; no instance was contacted and no implementation decision is made.\\n\\n### API State\\n\\n- Itsaplan product release is v1.5.0, while its runner package at that release is `@itsaplan/runner` v0.5.3, not v1.5.0. The versioned package metadata requires Node >=20 and exposes `itsaplan-runner`. [Sources: https://api.github.com/repos/croffasia/itsaplan/releases/tags/v1.5.0 ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/v1.5.0/packages/runner/package.json]\\n- External-run lifecycle: the agent-key-authenticated runner calls `POST /agent-runs/claim`, then heartbeat and result endpoints. A claim has `id`, `trigger`, framed prompt, system prompt, attempts, and nullable issue IDs. Result status is `success` or `failed`. [Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/apps/api/src/modules/agents/runner/index.ts ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/apps/api/src/modules/agents/runner/model.ts]\\n- Claim increments attempts and applies a server lease; heartbeat extends it. The server uses `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`; a no-result run is claimable after lease expiry, max-attempt exhaustion becomes failed, and a reported failure is terminal rather than retried. [Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/apps/api/src/modules/agents/runner/service.ts]\\n- The runner heartbeats every 60 seconds. It starts a detached process group, kills it on `timeoutMs`, reports exit code 0 as success, and reports nonzero/timeout outcomes as failed. A second termination signal leaves in-flight commands running; source states their leases expire and the runs are retried. v1.5.0 release notes separately state time/step-limit failures have no retry. [Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/v1.5.0/packages/runner/src/cli.ts ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/packages/runner/src/execute.ts ; https://api.github.com/repos/croffasia/itsaplan/releases/tags/v1.5.0]\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility\\n\\n- The official runner is an operator-machine queue consumer: it polls an Itsaplan instance and starts its selected CLI in configured `cwd`. Its configuration documents `cwd`, agent/command, args, environment, concurrency, poll interval, and timeout, but no worktree or branch setting. [Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/packages/runner/README.md]\\n- Presets cover Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Antigravity, Copilot, Pi, and OMP. Unattended postures differ: Claude uses `--permission-mode auto`; Codex uses `sandbox_mode=workspace-write`; Copilot uses `--allow-all-tools --no-ask-user`; OMP uses `--auto-approve`. The coding-agent CLI must be installed and signed in on that runner machine. [Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/packages/runner/src/presets.ts ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/docs/runner.md]\\n- The API key resolves to an external agent; non-agent and internal-agent callers are rejected. Project MCP must be enabled and uses that key for coding-agent access. [Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/apps/api/src/modules/agents/runner-auth.ts ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/packages/runner/README.md]\\n- Sources inspected establish queue consumption and documented triggers, but not a supported API or MCP operation that enqueues one specifically identified external-agent run from a development-harness dispatch item. This is an evidence gap, not a claim that no such upstream API exists.\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\n- Measured with `wc -c`: `dh_core/operations.py` 92,171 bytes, `backlog_core/dispatch_state.py` 28,174 bytes, and `skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py` 45,270 bytes: 165,615 bytes total. Conservative baseline estimates are bytes \u00f7 3.5: about 26,335, 8,050, and 12,934 tokens; these are not injected payloads.\\n- npm registry reports runner v0.5.3 unpacked size 60,253 bytes. No source measured queue latency or an end-to-end dispatch benchmark. [Source: https://registry.npmjs.org/@itsaplan/runner/latest]\\n- No delta can be measured because #4143 names no concrete changed artifact.\\n\\n### Codebase Audit\\n\\n- Local dispatch identity is `(milestone, wave_num, issue)`, the SQLite item primary key. The record holds PID, Claude session ID, timestamps, result/error text, cost, and result/error-file paths. [Sources: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py:71-104`; `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:2224-2271`]\\n- `dispatch_spawn` launches each item through `asyncio.create_subprocess_exec`, records PID/session ID from spawn JSON, and treats a nonempty result file as completion. It marks failed on launch error, non-JSON output, or a dead PID; stale recovery uses `os.kill(pid, 0)`. [Sources: `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/operations.py:1852-2059`; `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py:242-458`]\\n- Its static launch command names `dispatch-{milestone}-{issue}`, conditionally includes `--worktree` and `--branch`, and uses GitHub issue number/title as task text. It omits the required `spawn` subcommand; the target parser requires a subcommand and defines neither `--worktree` nor `--branch`. Static evidence therefore does not establish a functioning local end-to-end spawn lifecycle. [Sources: `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/operations.py:1802-1824`; `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py:1154-1232`]\\n- Kage-bunshin launches Claude in an isolated worktree and tmux session when its `spawn` subcommand is used. Itsaplan instead starts a CLI in configured `cwd`; upstream supplies no worktree/branch behavior. [Sources: `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py:692-786`; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/croffasia/itsaplan/main/packages/runner/README.md]\\n- #4142 is an open, separate feature for an Itsaplan `WorkItemBackend`. Neither item declares a dependency, so its necessity for #4143 is not established. [Source: https://github.com/Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills/issues/4142]\\n\\n### Cross-Angle Signals\\n\\n| Finding | Angles | Confidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Itsaplan runner is an operator-machine queue consumer; development-harness dispatch is a local subprocess/PID/result-file lifecycle. | API + Ecosystem + Codebase | High |\\n| Upstream run identity (`run.id`, agent key, optional Itsaplan issue) and local identity (`milestone`, `wave_num`, GitHub issue) differ; no mapping is defined. | API + Codebase | High |\\n| Completion evidence differs: upstream reports success/failed; local code detects a nonempty result file and PID liveness. | API + Codebase | High |\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\n[RESOLVED] The target phrase `@itsaplan/runner v1.5.0` conflates product and package versions. Evidence identifies product v1.5.0 and runner v0.5.3. No behavioral-source conflict was found.\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\n- No supported enqueue operation for a specified development-harness wave/item was established.\\n- No authoritative mapping exists for development-harness milestone/wave/GitHub issue versus Itsaplan project/issue/agent/run identities.\\n- No facts identify the instance, runner machine, agent-key owner, enabled MCP project, coding-agent sign-in, or permitted unattended posture.\\n- No external repository/worktree/branch boundary is decided.\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- Local static source exposes no `dispatch_spawn` cancellation endpoint or explicit retry operation; recovery is PID-based.\\n- No quantitative end-to-end benchmark or payload-size baseline for a specific #4143 change was available.\\n- #4142 necessity for #4143 is not established.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Offers\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nresearch/task-management/itsaplan.md, \\\"Key Features > AI agents\\\": an external agent runs on the operator's machine through `@itsaplan/runner`, invoked with `npx -y @itsaplan/runner` (entry, Installation & Usage). The runner \\\"polls the instance per agent key\\\" and \\\"gives each queued task to a coding agent CLI\\\" (packages/runner/README.md, as quoted in the entry). Its presets include `claude`, `codex`, and `copilot`. A task's result is read from the exit code: \\\"`0` is a success, and any other code is a failure\\\" (entry, Technical Architecture, Execution model). Queued runs start \\\"on an @mention in a comment, on an assignment, or on a schedule\\\" (entry, Problem Addressed). The REST API is documented in the same entry (see the sibling issue on the backlog backend).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Touches\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe dh dispatch path. `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/operations.py:1789` sets `_SPAWN_SCRIPT` to `skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py`. `_build_spawn_cmd` at `dh_core/operations.py:1802-1824` builds `[\\\"uv\\\", \\\"run\\\", spawn.py, \\\"--model\\\", model, \\\"--name\\\", \\\"dispatch-{milestone}-{issue_num}\\\"]`, adds `--worktree` for the work phase and `--branch` for the integration branch, and appends `Work on issue #{issue_num}: {item_title}` as the task text.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Read: `research/task-management/itsaplan.md` lines 47-55 (AI agents list) and 84-86 (Execution model for external agents).\\n- Read: `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/operations.py` lines 1785-1824.\\n- Grep `_SPAWN_SCRIPT|_build_spawn_cmd|spawn.py` in `plugins/development-harness`: confirmed the spawn call site named above.\\n- GitHub issue search `repo:Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills dispatch spawn runner agent`: no issue covering a queued-run or external-runner dispatch path was returned.\\n- Semantic search tools (ccc, graphify) were not run in this scan. Grep and Glob were the search tools used.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Could Change\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`dispatch spawn` could place each work item as a queued run through the itsaplan REST API, and an `@itsaplan/runner` process on the operator's machine would execute it, in place of a direct `uv run spawn.py` child process. Setup cost: a running itsaplan instance, an agent key per runner, a runner process per machine, and a change to `_build_spawn_cmd`'s caller. The entry documents that the presets pass unattended-run flags: \\\"Claude Code receives `--permission-mode auto`\\\" (entry, AI agents). That changes the permission model for dispatched work, and the change would need its own review before adoption.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"0db0bccc69f3511f055100ef3d74299a87a7138a387414d152c783c3a6ffc263\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABc2hiJg\",\"fields\":{\"close_comment\":\"\",\"suggested_location\":\"\",\"followup_to\":\"\",\"source\":\"Not specified\",\"stack\":\"\",\"groomed\":\"2026-10-17\",\"research_first\":\"\",\"topic\":\"\",\"language\":\"\",\"close_reason\":\"\",\"plan\":\"\",\"status\":\"needs-grooming\",\"layer\":\"\",\"files\":\"\",\"close_reference\":\"\",\"added\":\"2026-10-17\",\"priority\":\"P2\",\"item_type\":\"Feature\"}}"}